  "intro": {
    "title": "Welcome",
    "viewOnlineLink": "https://nearfuturelaboratory.com/newsletters/2025/w47-y25/",
    "content": "<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">Hello from Dubai, where I'll be for the week split between two events: the Dubai Future Forum Tues - Wed, and then Thurs - Fri in Abu Dhabi at MBZUAI for a symposium on the Future of Human-Centered AI. <br/> I had mentioned Harvard-Westlake and by coincidence (I guess?) that was followed by a workshop with students at Chapman University and then a few hours before heading to Dubai, 5 hours of one-on-one's with students in Liam Young's Fiction & Entertainment studio at SCI-Arc. <br/> #  blog, post or LinkedIn post on the anti-aesthetic AI thing I think there’s something good there. #  a word of the week hyperstition or heard on the street referring back to Radha and Tobias’s podcast episode with hyperstition￼￼ #  lead for the anti-aesthetic refer back to University of Washington hit Lab, Paul Berger, and my introduction to aesthetics and theory and postmodernism ￼ <br/></p>"
  },
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "sponsor",
      "title": "Principles of Promotion",
      "sponsorLink": null,
      "sponsorLabel": "PROMOTE",
      "items": [
        {
          "title": "Principles",
          "link": "https://nearfuturelaboratory.com/blog/2025/11/promotional-principles-for-creative-people/",
          "description": "<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0;    ; font-family: IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif !important; font-size: 19px !important; line-height: 1.2 !important; color: #111111 !important; text-align: left !important\">Metalabel's practical guide for creative people who love making work but struggle with promotion. It offers twelve principles such things as “do it *for* yourself” and “small is beautiful” to things like release windowing, hosting events, and making the next thing. All of this reframes marketing as care, context, and community rather than chasing metrics. It's got examples, anecdotes, and contributions from other creators (me too!), it argues for defining your own version of success, protecting your creative joy, and staying devoted to the long-term practice of making. It seems to still exist as a living/evolving Google doc.</p>",
          "subtitle": "PROMOTE OR PERISH",
          "image": "https://imagedelivery.net/gaLGizR3kCgx5yRLtiRIOw/4df44302-f519-40f1-c3a1-b66d682ca700/w=600",
